<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Frederick, Duke of York and Albany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1763">1763</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Frederick, Duke of York</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Frederick, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and Albany, was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>. As an infant he was made prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, which he held until it was secularised in 1802. He later commanded British armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and, as commander-in-chief, carried out important reforms of the British Army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-frederick-duke-of-york-and-albany.jpg" alt="Prince Frederick, Duke of York and Albany" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Frederick,_Duke_of_York_-_Lawrence_1816.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
